1
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:11,353
Twenty races. Six different winners. One season like no other.

2
00:00:13,103 --> 00:00:17,265
A twenty year old became the youngest world champion in history.

3
00:00:19,015 --> 00:00:21,888
Lewis Hamilton finally won in red.

4
00:00:23,638 --> 00:00:27,818
Max Verstappen went from twenty first on the grid, to second.

5
00:00:29,568 --> 00:00:33,928
Two races were cancelled. One was moved to a different continent.

6
00:00:35,678 --> 00:00:40,265
And one podium changed hands three times, after the race had finished.

7
00:00:42,015 --> 00:00:45,757
New cars. New engines. New teams. New champion.

8
00:00:47,507 --> 00:00:52,889
This is the 2026 Formula One season, so far. Buckle up.

9
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:11,750
First things first. 2026 wasn't just a new season. It was basically a new sport.

10
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,737
The chassis rules were rewritten from scratch.

11
00:01:14,887 --> 00:01:19,199
The cars got shorter, narrower, and around thirty kilos lighter.

12
00:01:19,349 --> 00:01:23,390
The engines now get almost half of their power from the battery,

13
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:27,227
and they run on one hundred percent sustainable fuel.

14
00:01:31,666 --> 00:01:35,931
The wings now move. Open on the straights, closed in the corners.

15
00:01:36,081 --> 00:01:39,919
And DRS? Retired, after fifteen years of service.

16
00:01:40,069 --> 00:01:47,088
In its place: Overtake Mode, an extra shot of electric power when you're within one second of the car ahead.

17
00:01:47,238 --> 00:01:52,865
So Formula One became a very fast, very expensive game of battery management.

18
00:01:53,015 --> 00:01:58,560
Basically, it's your phone at eleven percent. But at three hundred kilometres an hour.

19
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:09,396
The grid grew to eleven teams. Cadillac arrived, with Sergio Perez and Valtteri Bottas.

20
00:02:09,546 --> 00:02:15,597
Sauber became Audi. Honda joined Aston Martin, where Adrian Newey now runs the show.

21
00:02:15,747 --> 00:02:19,294
Red Bull started building its own engines, with Ford.

22
00:02:19,444 --> 00:02:26,188
And Isack Hadjar got promoted to the hardest job in Formula One: being Max Verstappen's team-mate.

23
00:02:26,538 --> 00:02:33,441
Ferrari kept Hamilton and Leclerc, and McLaren started the year with the reigning champion, Lando Norris.

24
00:02:37,899 --> 00:02:42,955
The calendar had twenty four races, including a brand new track in Madrid.

25
00:02:43,105 --> 00:02:50,033
But one week into the season, the conflict in the Middle East forced Bahrain and Saudi Arabia to be cancelled.

26
00:02:50,183 --> 00:02:54,917
Later in the year, the Bahrain Grand Prix was rescheduled. In Malaysia.

27
00:02:55,067 --> 00:03:01,332
Yes. The Bahrain Grand Prix. In Malaysia. Next year: the British Grand Prix, live from the Moon.

28
00:03:01,682 --> 00:03:05,413
In the end: twenty three rounds. Twenty done, three to go.

29
00:03:14,366 --> 00:03:20,069
Round one, Melbourne. George Russell took pole and the win, leading a Mercedes one-two.

30
00:03:20,219 --> 00:03:25,760
Charles Leclerc fought hard, but Ferrari's strategy let him down, and he finished third.

31
00:03:25,910 --> 00:03:30,356
And Oscar Piastri, at his home race, crashed on the way to the grid.

32
00:03:30,506 --> 00:03:34,204
Zero laps. At home. In front of his family. Ouch.

33
00:03:38,866 --> 00:03:42,040
Then Shanghai, and the arrival of a superstar.

34
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:48,116
Kimi Antonelli, nineteen years old, took pole and his first ever Grand Prix victory.

35
00:03:48,266 --> 00:03:54,222
The second youngest winner in history, and the first Italian winner since 2006.

36
00:03:54,372 --> 00:03:58,812
And Lewis Hamilton finally got his first Grand Prix podium in red.

37
00:04:03,266 --> 00:04:10,127
Suzuka. Antonelli again, from pole again, helped by a safety car after Oliver Bearman's crash.

38
00:04:10,277 --> 00:04:14,692
That made him the youngest championship leader in Formula One history.

39
00:04:14,842 --> 00:04:20,519
Leading the world championship at nineteen. At nineteen, I couldn't lead a group project.

40
00:04:25,166 --> 00:04:28,987
After a five week break, Miami. Antonelli, from pole.

41
00:04:29,137 --> 00:04:32,636
Montreal. Antonelli, again. You get the idea.

42
00:04:32,786 --> 00:04:36,846
Monaco. Antonelli, by six seconds, from Lewis Hamilton.

43
00:04:36,996 --> 00:04:43,304
Five Grand Prix wins in a row. At this point the others were just there for the free hotel rooms.

44
00:04:47,966 --> 00:04:51,839
After six rounds, Mercedes had won six out of six.

45
00:04:51,989 --> 00:04:56,220
Antonelli had won five of them. And he was still only nineteen.

46
00:05:04,866 --> 00:05:10,217
Every great season needs a rivalry. The first one was inside Mercedes.

47
00:05:10,367 --> 00:05:18,758
George Russell started the year as team leader and title favourite. He won in Melbourne, then watched his team-mate win five in a row.

48
00:05:18,908 --> 00:05:27,918
Russell kept fighting, with wins in Austria and Baku. But his luck kept running out, like at Spa, where his race lasted less than a lap.

49
00:05:28,068 --> 00:05:32,151
Nine wins to three. Team-mates, they said. Friends, they said.

50
00:05:36,166 --> 00:05:40,735
Barcelona, round seven. The moment Ferrari fans had waited for.

51
00:05:40,885 --> 00:05:43,919
Lewis Hamilton won his first race in red.

52
00:05:44,069 --> 00:05:52,028
At forty one, the oldest winner since Jack Brabham in 1970. Career win number one hundred and six.

53
00:05:52,178 --> 00:05:56,946
Antonelli retired, and suddenly Hamilton was second in the championship.

54
00:05:57,096 --> 00:06:02,491
Everybody who said Lewis was finished is now very quietly deleting their posts.

55
00:06:06,533 --> 00:06:14,134
Two weeks later in Austria, George Russell hit back, winning at the Red Bull Ring to keep his own title hopes alive.

56
00:06:14,284 --> 00:06:20,391
Then Silverstone. Antonelli started from pole, but a wheel problem dropped him out of the points.

57
00:06:20,541 --> 00:06:27,193
Charles Leclerc pounced, and won the British Grand Prix ahead of the two home heroes, Russell and Hamilton.

58
00:06:27,343 --> 00:06:32,828
Antonelli did get one consolation that weekend: his first ever sprint win.

59
00:06:36,666 --> 00:06:39,339
Inside Ferrari, it's razor close.

60
00:06:39,489 --> 00:06:47,329
Leclerc won at Silverstone, his first British Grand Prix win, after pole sitter Antonelli was hit by a wheel problem.

61
00:06:47,479 --> 00:06:53,581
One win each, separated by just twelve points. And in Mexico, they even clashed with each other.

62
00:06:53,731 --> 00:06:57,163
The team radio that day must have been... interesting.

63
00:07:01,199 --> 00:07:05,691
At Spa, Antonelli held off a late attack from Leclerc to win again.

64
00:07:05,841 --> 00:07:10,019
Then the reigning champion woke up. Lando Norris won in Hungary,

65
00:07:10,169 --> 00:07:12,973
and again at Zandvoort. Two in a row.

66
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:24,159
At McLaren, last year's title fight has become a one way street. Norris: three wins. Piastri: none.

67
00:07:24,309 --> 00:07:30,293
A crash in Melbourne, a gearbox failure in Hungary, and a lot of days in his team-mate's shadow.

68
00:07:30,443 --> 00:07:35,331
Oscar, if you're watching: 2027 is a new year, mate.

69
00:07:39,366 --> 00:07:45,162
But Antonelli always had an answer. At Monza, his home race, he started nineteenth.

70
00:07:45,312 --> 00:07:51,823
And he won. The first Italian to win the Italian Grand Prix since 1966.

71
00:07:51,973 --> 00:07:56,712
Italy lost its mind. Somewhere, an Italian grandmother is still crying.

72
00:08:00,733 --> 00:08:04,142
The brand new track in Madrid: Antonelli again.

73
00:08:04,292 --> 00:08:09,040
Baku: Russell beat Verstappen by zero point one nine six seconds.

74
00:08:09,190 --> 00:08:11,497
That's faster than you can blink.

75
00:08:11,847 --> 00:08:16,579
And then Max woke up. Sepang, in the rain: his first win of the year.

76
00:08:16,729 --> 00:08:21,929
Singapore: the sprint and the Grand Prix. Both his. The old champion was back.

77
00:08:25,766 --> 00:08:29,166
Let's zoom in on Sepang, because it was a big one.

78
00:08:29,316 --> 00:08:35,710
Rain at the start, a late safety car, and Verstappen in control: pole, fastest lap, and the win.

79
00:08:35,860 --> 00:08:42,120
The seventy second victory of his career, and the very first for Red Bull's own Ford backed engine.

80
00:08:42,270 --> 00:08:47,295
Seven months without a win. For Max, that's basically a mid-life crisis.

81
00:08:55,833 --> 00:09:02,882
In Austin, Antonelli was untouchable. Pole, victory, thirteen seconds clear. His ninth win of the year.

82
00:09:03,032 --> 00:09:07,853
Going to Mexico, he only needed ninth place to become world champion.

83
00:09:11,666 --> 00:09:17,812
Mexico City. Chaos, penalties, and Max Verstappen winning again, with Hamilton second.

84
00:09:17,962 --> 00:09:22,950
But all eyes were on the Mercedes in third place. Antonelli brought it home.

85
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:24,628
And that was enough.

86
00:09:28,466 --> 00:09:33,086
Kimi Antonelli. Formula One World Champion, at twenty years old.

87
00:09:33,236 --> 00:09:43,387
The youngest champion in history, beating Sebastian Vettel's record. The first Italian champion since Alberto Ascari in 1953.

88
00:09:43,537 --> 00:09:49,742
Only the fourth driver ever to win the title in his second season. And with four races to spare.

89
00:09:49,892 --> 00:09:56,678
Fun fact: in some countries, he's still too young to rent a car. But he's old enough to be world champion.

90
00:10:04,900 --> 00:10:08,487
Let's talk drama. And it started before the first race.

91
00:10:08,637 --> 00:10:17,024
Rivals suspected the Mercedes engine went over the sixteen to one compression limit when hot, while passing the test when cold.

92
00:10:17,174 --> 00:10:25,488
Mercedes said it was completely legal. The engine makers agreed a compromise: from June, engines would also be checked hot.

93
00:10:25,638 --> 00:10:29,847
Mercedes then won both championships anyway. Awkward.

94
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:41,589
Then, Monaco's never ending story. Pierre Gasly finished third for Alpine.

95
00:10:41,739 --> 00:10:46,002
Penalties dropped him to seventh. A review gave him the podium back.

96
00:10:46,152 --> 00:10:53,993
Then McLaren and Red Bull appealed, and in September, the Court of Appeal took it away again. Isack Hadjar got the trophy.

97
00:10:54,143 --> 00:10:59,539
Third, seventh, third, seventh. Pierre has had more ups and downs than a theme park.

98
00:11:10,666 --> 00:11:14,048
Now, the crashes. Nothing too scary, I promise.

99
00:11:14,198 --> 00:11:20,819
Melbourne: Oscar Piastri lost his McLaren on the way to the grid, before the race had even started.

100
00:11:27,266 --> 00:11:35,110
Suzuka: a big crash for Oliver Bearman at Spoon curve. The safety car came out, and the race was turned upside down.

101
00:11:41,566 --> 00:11:47,867
Spa, lap one: Hamilton and Russell made contact. Russell was out. Hamilton was penalised.

102
00:11:48,017 --> 00:11:53,491
Lewis and George. Former team-mates. Still sharing everything, including the gravel.

103
00:12:00,133 --> 00:12:05,971
Zandvoort: Max Verstappen crashed heavily on the opening lap of his home race. Red flag.

104
00:12:06,121 --> 00:12:10,708
Thankfully he was fine, and within six weeks, he was winning races again.

105
00:12:17,133 --> 00:12:23,322
Baku: Franco Colapinto's Alpine took out his own team-mate Pierre Gasly, and Lando Norris.

106
00:12:23,472 --> 00:12:27,398
Two cars with one move. You have to admire the efficiency.

107
00:12:34,033 --> 00:12:39,557
And Mexico: the two Ferraris, Hamilton and Leclerc, clashed in front of the whole world.

108
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,202
And then there was the bad luck.

109
00:12:48,352 --> 00:12:52,156
Barcelona: Antonelli retired while fighting for second.

110
00:12:52,306 --> 00:12:57,348
Silverstone: from pole position to no points, after a wheel shield failure.

111
00:12:57,498 --> 00:13:01,421
Spa: Russell's race was over before the end of lap one.

112
00:13:01,571 --> 00:13:06,895
Hungary: Piastri, out with a gearbox failure after contact with Carlos Sainz.

113
00:13:07,045 --> 00:13:12,725
Formula One: where your car can cost more than a house, and still break like a cheap umbrella.

114
00:13:23,866 --> 00:13:29,150
The biggest surprises? Number one: Lewis Hamilton, at forty one, winning again.

115
00:13:29,300 --> 00:13:36,097
Number two: Isack Hadjar, with podiums in Monaco, Baku and Brazil, in the toughest seat in the sport.

116
00:13:36,247 --> 00:13:41,121
Number three: rookie Arvid Lindblad, scoring points on his debut in Melbourne.

117
00:13:41,271 --> 00:13:48,287
And a bonus: Ferrari won two races this year, after not winning a single one in 2025.

118
00:13:48,437 --> 00:13:53,942
And number four: the Bahrain Grand Prix. In Malaysia. Sorry. I'm still not over it.

119
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:11,897
Drive of the year? Brazil. In tricky wet and dry conditions, Max Verstappen started twenty first.

120
00:14:12,047 --> 00:14:19,047
He finished second. Lando Norris won from pole, his second Interlagos win in a row, with Hadjar third.

121
00:14:19,197 --> 00:14:22,580
Max treated the starting grid like a suggestion.

122
00:14:29,233 --> 00:14:32,315
And the biggest disappointment? Aston Martin.

123
00:14:32,465 --> 00:14:37,657
Adrian Newey. Honda power. A brand new factory. Huge expectations.

124
00:14:37,807 --> 00:14:39,799
And seven points. Seven.

125
00:14:46,433 --> 00:14:51,059
Cadillac, in their first ever season, are still waiting for their first point.

126
00:14:51,209 --> 00:14:54,079
But in fairness, they look fantastic doing it.

127
00:15:04,933 --> 00:15:13,268
Mercedes: champions. They sealed the constructors' title in Brazil: their ninth, and their first since 2021.

128
00:15:13,418 --> 00:15:19,112
Twelve wins from twenty races. They nailed the new rules. Rating: ten out of ten.

129
00:15:22,766 --> 00:15:29,108
Kimi Antonelli: nine wins, three hundred and ninety points, and a calm head way beyond his age.

130
00:15:29,258 --> 00:15:34,281
Rating: ten out of ten. I'd trust him with my car. If he could rent one.

131
00:15:38,133 --> 00:15:44,400
Max Verstappen: a slow start with Red Bull's brand new engine, and seven months without a win.

132
00:15:44,550 --> 00:15:51,001
Then three wins in the autumn, and right now, second in the championship. Never, ever count him out.

133
00:15:54,633 --> 00:16:01,363
Lewis Hamilton: his first Ferrari win, a record age, and only eighteen points from second place.

134
00:16:01,513 --> 00:16:05,457
After a tough first year in red, this was the comeback season.

135
00:16:05,607 --> 00:16:10,898
Rating: eight and a half. Forty one years old, and still faster than my wifi.

136
00:16:14,733 --> 00:16:20,330
George Russell: three wins, and the man who opened the new era with victory in Melbourne.

137
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:27,315
Fast all year, but unlucky at the worst moments, and beaten by a team-mate who just wouldn't stop winning.

138
00:16:27,465 --> 00:16:33,716
Rating: eight out of ten. Plus two extra points for keeping a smile in the Mercedes garage.

139
00:16:37,566 --> 00:16:45,592
Charles Leclerc: a dream win at Silverstone, plenty of podiums, and a team-mate battle that is basically a coin toss.

140
00:16:49,233 --> 00:16:57,074
Lando Norris: a slow start, then three wins. And McLaren are just three points behind Red Bull in the fight for third.

141
00:17:00,700 --> 00:17:05,100
Isack Hadjar: one hundred and twenty two points and three podiums.

142
00:17:05,250 --> 00:17:09,390
Many drivers have been destroyed by that seat. Isack wasn't.

143
00:17:13,233 --> 00:17:20,870
Oscar Piastri: a year ago, he was fighting for the title. In 2026, he's still waiting for a win.

144
00:17:21,020 --> 00:17:26,144
A podium in Suzuka, but too many bad days. He's too good for this to last.

145
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:37,082
Racing Bulls: best of the rest. Liam Lawson has eighty nine points, and rookie Lindblad has been impressive too.

146
00:17:37,232 --> 00:17:42,467
Fifth in the constructors' championship, and comfortably ahead of the midfield pack.

147
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:54,215
Aston Martin: the dream team, on paper. Fernando Alonso and Lance Stroll have spent most of the year fighting at the back.

148
00:17:54,365 --> 00:17:56,906
Rating: we'll check again next year.

149
00:18:09,233 --> 00:18:14,494
The drivers' championship. Antonelli, champion, on three hundred and ninety points.

150
00:18:14,644 --> 00:18:23,002
Behind him, five drivers within thirty three points, fighting for second: Verstappen, Russell, Hamilton, Leclerc and Norris.

151
00:18:30,333 --> 00:18:36,260
The constructors: Mercedes, champions, on six hundred and fifty two. Ferrari second.

152
00:18:36,410 --> 00:18:42,030
Red Bull and McLaren, separated by three points. And Cadillac, still on zero.

153
00:18:49,366 --> 00:18:52,646
And the wins: twenty races, six different winners.

154
00:18:52,796 --> 00:18:59,067
Antonelli nine. Russell, Verstappen and Norris three each. Hamilton and Leclerc, one apiece.

155
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:10,184
Three races left: Las Vegas, Qatar, and Abu Dhabi.

156
00:19:10,334 --> 00:19:15,495
The titles are gone, but second place, in both championships, is wide open.

157
00:19:15,645 --> 00:19:21,537
Can Verstappen hold on to second? Can McLaren beat Red Bull? We'll find out very soon.

158
00:19:28,866 --> 00:19:35,074
2026. A new era, new cars, new teams, and a brand new champion.

159
00:19:35,224 --> 00:19:39,730
A twenty year old from Bologna, who beat the very best in the world.

160
00:19:39,880 --> 00:19:43,050
Who's your driver of the year? Tell me in the comments.

161
00:19:43,200 --> 00:19:46,187
Hit subscribe, and I'll see you in Las Vegas.

